[CourseId "caro-kann-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Caro-Kann Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "A solid, well-signposted answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-advance vs-exchange vs-panov vs-fantasy vs-two-knights light-squares c5-break isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterId "ch2-advance"]
[ChapterTitle "The Advance: 3.e5"]
[DefaultTags "vs-advance"]

{White grabs space and locks the centre. This is the most common answer you will meet below club strength, and it is also the one where the Caro-Kann shows why it is not the French.

## Get the bishop out first

3...Bf5 is the move. The pawn on e5 has shut the door on the a2-g8 diagonal but not on the b1-h7 one, so the bishop steps outside before ...e6 arrives. A French player in this structure spends the middlegame trying to free that bishop. You have it out on move three.

## Then hit the base

White's pawn chain runs d4 to e5. The base is d4, and the lever is ...c5. So the plan is short: ...e6, develop the pieces behind the pawns, and play ...c5 at the first sensible moment. If the centre opens, your pieces are better placed than White's. Later on ...f6 is the second lever, hitting the head of the chain once d4 has been dealt with.

## The aggressive tries

Many opponents play 4.Nf3 and 5.Be2 and simply develop. Sharper ones play 4.Nc3 followed by 5.g4, throwing pawns at your bishop. It looks frightening and it costs White something real: those pawns cannot come back, and the White king has nowhere safe to go afterwards. Answer it with ...Bg6, ...c5 and ...h6, keep your head, and let White explain what the attack was for.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing ...e6 before ...Bf5 and getting the French position the whole opening avoids. Answering 4.h4 with ...h6, when ...h5 stops the pawn dead. Grabbing on g4 when the pawn is offered, which opens lines at your own king for one pawn. And the big one: getting a comfortable position and never playing ...c5, so White keeps the space advantage for free.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5
{Out before the door shuts. This is the move the whole defence is built around. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 3... c5!?
{The Botvinnik-Carls, hitting d4 at once. Sound, and much less theory, but it gives up the free bishop that makes the Caro-Kann worth playing.}
) ( 3... e6?!
{This is a French Defence with the bishop locked in for the rest of the game, which is the one position ...c6 was played to avoid.}
) 4. Nf3 {Quiet development, and by far the most common choice at club level. [%weight 6]}
( 4. Nc3 {The sharp choice. White intends g4, hunting the bishop with pawns. [%weight 5]}
4... e6
{Build the wall. The bishop is already outside, so there is nothing to fear from a closed centre. [%tags development]}
( 4... Qb6!?
{It hits b2 and d4, but the queen comes out too early and Na4 gains time on her.} ) 5. g4
{The Short and Tal try. White throws the g-pawn forward to drive the bishop back. [%weight 5]}
( 5. Be3
{The restrained version: White defends d4 and leaves the pawns alone. [%weight 4]}
5... Ne7 {The knight goes behind the pawns, heading for g6 or f5. [%tags development]}
6. Nge2 {Simple development. [%weight 5]} 6... Nd7
{The last minor piece gets a square, and c5 is coming next. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...c5 and ...Qb6, hitting d4 and b2 together. If White ever trades on c5, recapture with a piece and use the half open c-file.] [%line 4.Nc3 with the quiet 5.Be3]}
) 5... Bg6
{Step back and stay outside the chain. Every pawn White has thrown forward is a weakness once the game opens. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 5... Be4?
{Nothing hangs, but 6.Nxe4 dxe4 gives up the bishop this whole opening exists to free, and leaves Black with doubled e-pawns against the two bishops.}
) 6. Nge2
{The knight develops behind the pawns, heading for f4 to hit the bishop again. [%weight 6]}
6... c5
{Counter in the centre. While White chases a bishop on the wing, Black attacks the base of the chain. [%tags c5-break]}
( 6... h5?!
{It opens lines on the side where White has more pawns. Answer a wing attack in the centre.}
) 7. h4 {White keeps going, threatening h5 to trap the bishop. [%weight 6]} 7... h6
{Now the pawn is needed: it gives the bishop the h7 square and stops h5 from arriving with a threat. [%tags king-safety]}
( 7... h5?!
{7...h5 8.gxh5 opens the g-file straight at Black's own king with the bishop still on g6.}
) 8. Be3 {White finally defends d4 and connects the pieces. [%weight 5]} 8... Nc6
{Pile on d4. White's king has no safe square left on either wing. [%tags development] [%plan Take on d4 when it helps, develop with ...Nge7 and ...Qb6, and aim at the weakened light squares. White has spent five moves on pawn moves and still has to find a home for the king.] [%line 4.Nc3 and the g4 push]}
) ( 4. Bd3
{White offers the trade at once, hoping the queen on d3 will be useful on the diagonal. [%weight 4]}
4... Bxd3
{Take. The bishop has already done its job by getting out, and White's queen now has to do the work of a bishop. [%tags light-squares]}
( 4... Bg6?!
{5.Bxg6 hxg6 hands Black the open h-file but a shaky pawn front. The straight trade is cleaner.}
) ( 4... e6!?
{Also fine. Taking first means White never gets to choose the moment of the trade.} )
5. Qxd3 {The queen recaptures and eyes h7 and the queenside at once. [%weight 9]} 5... e6
{Build the structure. Nothing is blocked in now that the bishop is off the board. [%tags development]}
( 5... c5!?
{Playing the break at once is fine too, though ...e6 first means the bishop on f8 always has a square.}
) 6. Nf3 {Development, and the knight supports d4. [%weight 6]} 6... c5
{On schedule: hit the base of the chain. [%tags c5-break]} ( 6... Ne7!?
{Good development, and the break can wait a move. There is no reason to let White consolidate with c3 first.}
) 7. O-O {White castles and leaves the centre for later. [%weight 5]} 7... Nc6
{Develop with pressure on d4. [%tags development]} 8. c3
{White shores up the chain. [%weight 5]} 8... Nge7
{The last knight comes out behind the pawns, heading for g6 or f5. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, then decide between ...f6 against e5 and ...Qb6 against d4 and b2. With the light-squared bishops gone, White has no attack to build.] [%line 4.Bd3, trading at once]}
) ( 4. h4
{A club favourite: throw the h-pawn at the bishop before developing anything. [%weight 3]}
4... h5
{Stop it dead. With the pawn on h5 the bishop can never be driven off the diagonal, and White has spent a move weakening the kingside. [%tags king-safety]}
( 4... h6?!
{5.h5 comes anyway and the bishop is pushed back to h7 for nothing. Here the pawn belongs on h5.}
) 5. Bd3 {White gives up on the pawn hunt and trades instead. [%weight 5]} 5... Bxd3
{Take, and note that White's h-pawn is now a long-term weakness. [%tags light-squares]}
6. Qxd3 {The queen recaptures. [%weight 9]} 6... e6
{The usual structure. [%tags development]} 7. Ne2
{White develops the knight where it does not block the f-pawn. [%weight 5]} 7... c5
{The break, as always. [%tags c5-break] [%plan Develop with ...Nc6 and ...Nge7, castle short, and remember that the pawn on h4 means White cannot castle kingside comfortably.] [%line The 4.h4 chase]}
) ( 4. c4
{White claims the whole centre. It is ambitious, and it leaves d4 short of defenders. [%weight 2]}
4... e6
{Solid. Black finishes the structure and prepares to hit the overstretched centre. [%tags development]}
( 4... dxc4?!
{It hands White the centre for one pawn that is hard to hold, and Bxc4 develops with tempo.}
) 5. Nc3 {Development, and White defends the c-pawn a second time. [%weight 6]} 5... Ne7
{The knight goes to e7 so that ...c5 and ...Nbc6 come next without blocking anything. [%tags development]}
6. cxd5 {White releases the tension. [%weight 5]} 6... Nxd5
{Recapture with the knight. It lands in the centre with tempo on nothing in particular, and it leaves the pawn on c6 free to play ...c5 next. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...c5 next, hitting d4 with the c-pawn after all, then ...Nc6 and ...Be7. White's pawn on e5 has no supporters left.] [%line The 4.c4 space grab]}
( 6... cxd5?!
{It keeps the wall and gives up the ...c5 break, which is the move this whole chapter is built around.}
) ( 6... exd5?! {It buries the bishop that was just developed and hands White the e-file.}
) ) 4... e6
{Now the pawn comes, because the bishop is already outside it. [%tags development]} (
4... c5?!
{It hits d4 a move earlier and costs about a pawn: ...e6 first keeps the bishop on f8 supplied with squares, which is what makes the break work later.}
) ( 4... h6?! {There is no threat to stop yet, and the move does nothing for the plan.} )
5. Be2 {The main setup: develop, castle, and keep the extra space. [%weight 6]} ( 5. Bd3
{White offers the trade of light-squared bishops before Black is ready for it. [%weight 4]}
5... Bxd3
{Take. Black is happy to trade this bishop once it has done its job of getting out. [%tags light-squares]}
6. Qxd3 {The queen recaptures and looks at the kingside. [%weight 9]} 6... c5
{The lever, on schedule. [%tags c5-break]} 7. c3 {White holds d4 with a pawn. [%weight 6]}
7... Nc6
{Develop with pressure on d4. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Nge7 and ...Ng6, castle, and keep the pressure on d4 and e5. With the light-squared bishops off, White has no attacking piece left.] [%line 5.Bd3, the bishop trade]}
) ( 5. c3 {A quiet move keeping everything together. [%weight 3]} 5... c5
{The break comes anyway. White's extra pawn move has not defended d4 any better. [%tags c5-break]}
6. Be2 {Development, transposing to the main line of this chapter. [%weight 5]} 6... Nc6
{Develop and hit d4. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Nge7 and ...Ng6 and castle. This is the same structure as the 5.Be2 c5 6.c3 line, reached in a different order.] [%line The quiet 5.c3]}
) 5... c5
{The lever. White's chain rests on d4, so that is where the pawn goes. [%tags c5-break]} (
5... Ne7!?
{A good square for the knight, heading for g6 or c6. The break comes first while White is still uncastled.}
) ( 5... Nd7!?
{Solid development. Nothing wrong with it, but the pawn break is what changes the position.}
) 6. Be3 {White props up d4 with a piece rather than a pawn. [%weight 5]} ( 6. c3
{White holds the chain with a pawn and keeps everything closed. [%weight 4]} 6... Nc6
{Develop and add pressure to d4. There is no hurry to release the tension now that White has committed the c-pawn. [%tags development]}
( 6... cxd4!?
{Also fine. Developing first and taking once White has castled means the recapture comes at a worse moment for White.}
) 7. O-O {White castles and waits. [%weight 5]} 7... cxd4
{With White castled and the c-pawn committed, take. The recapture leaves the d-pawn on an open file with nothing behind it. [%tags c5-break]}
( 7... Nge7?!
{Natural, and it lets White keep the tension and play a3 and b4 with space on the queenside.}
) 8. cxd4
{Forced in effect: taking with a piece would leave the pawn on e5 hanging. [%weight 9]}
8... Nge7
{Now the knight comes out, heading for g6 or f5 where it hits the pawns White has left behind. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Ng6, ...Be7 and castle, then use the half open c-file and press d4. White has the space and the only two targets on the board.] [%line 6.c3, holding the centre]}
) ( 6. dxc5 {White releases the tension and hopes to hold the extra pawn. [%weight 3]}
6... Bxc5
{Take it straight back. The bishop reaches an excellent diagonal and Black is fully developed on the queenside. [%tags c5-break]}
7. O-O {White castles and gets on with it. [%weight 6]} 7... Ne7
{The knight heads for c6 or g6 without blocking anything. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, then play ...Nbc6 and ...Qb6 with pressure on d4 and f2. White has given up the centre for nothing.] [%line 6.dxc5, giving up the tension]}
) 6... cxd4
{Take now. Leaving the tension lets White play dxc5 at the moment that suits White. [%tags c5-break]}
( 6... Qb6!?
{It hits b2 and d4 together and is a real line. It also invites the queen to be chased around, which is more to know.}
) ( 6... Nc6!?
{Sensible, and it usually transposes. Taking first fixes the structure while it is favourable.}
) 7. Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight and keeps the pawn on e5. [%weight 8]}
7... Ne7
{The right square. From e7 the knight covers f5 and can go to c6 or g6, and it never blocks the c-file. [%tags development]}
( 7... Nc6!?
{Also natural. Putting this knight on e7 first leaves c6 free for the other one and keeps both bishops unblocked.}
) 8. O-O {White tucks the king away before the position opens further. [%weight 6]}
8... Nbc6
{The second knight joins, hitting the knight on d4 and eyeing the e5 pawn behind it. [%tags development]}
9. Bb5
{The best try. White pins the knight and trades it off before the pressure on e5 starts to tell. [%weight 6]}
( 9. Nxf5
{Natural, and it gives up e5. The knight that has just left the board was, through f3, the only piece defending that pawn. [%weight 5]}
9... Nxf5
{Recapture with the knight, which now hits e3 and d4 and is ready to help take on e5. [%tags light-squares] [%plan Nothing defends the pawn on e5, so take it. If White plays 10.c3 first, 10...Nxe3 is stronger still: chapter 6 runs that line to the end.] [%line White's 9.Nxf5 trade]}
( 9... exf5?
{It buries the light squares and lets White hold e5 in peace with f4. The engine puts it nearly two pawns behind the knight recapture.}
) ) 9... a6
{Ask the bishop to commit. While it sits on b5 the knight on c6 is pinned to the king and cannot touch e5. [%tags development]}
( 9... Qb6?
{It walks onto a discovered attack. The bishop on e3 is aimed at b6 through the knight, so 17.Nxf5 Nxf5 18.Bxb6 wins the queen for a bishop.}
) ( 9... Rc8!?
{The rook belongs there eventually, but the knight stays pinned for another move.} )
10. Bxc6+ {White takes rather than retreat and lose the pin for nothing. [%weight 7]}
10... Nxc6
{Recapture with the knight. It keeps the pawns whole and puts a defender back on e5 and d4. [%tags development]}
( 10... bxc6?!
{It doubles the pawns and blocks the b-file, for a c-file Black already half owns.} )
11. Nxf5
{White trades off the good bishop while the knight on c6 still guards e5. [%weight 6]}
11... exf5
{Take with the pawn here. The knight is needed on c6, and the pawn on f5 takes e4 away from White for good. [%tags light-squares] [%plan Play ...Be7 or ...Qb6 and castle, then hit e5 with ...f6. White has a little more space and no way to attack, which is the bargain the Caro-Kann always offers.] [%line The full main line, with 6.Be3]}
*


[CourseId "caro-kann-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Caro-Kann Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "A solid, well-signposted answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-advance vs-exchange vs-panov vs-fantasy vs-two-knights light-squares c5-break isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterId "ch3-exchange"]
[ChapterTitle "The Exchange: 3.exd5"]
[DefaultTags "vs-exchange"]

{White releases the tension straight away. After 3...cxd5 the pawns are symmetrical, the position is level, and both sides simply develop. This is the quietest chapter in the course and the one you are most likely to see from an opponent who does not know the Caro-Kann.

## Recapture with the c-pawn

3...cxd5 is automatic. It keeps a pawn on d5, opens the c-file for a rook and leaves the bishop on c8 free to come out. Taking with the queen instead invites 4.Nc3, which develops a piece and gains a move on her.

## What Black is playing for

The half open c-file. Black's rook belongs on c8, the knight on c6, and the pressure builds against c2 and the b-file. White has the same file on the other side and one extra move, so this is not a position to force. Develop properly, castle, put the rook on c8 and the position plays itself.

## The one line worth knowing

After 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.c3 Nf6 6.Bf4 Bg4 White often plays 7.Qb3, hitting b7 and looking at d5. The answer is 7...Na5, chasing the queen and covering everything at once. After 8.Qa4+ Bd7 9.Qc2 Black has gained time and stands perfectly well. Learn that one sequence and the rest of the chapter is common sense.

## Not this chapter

If White follows up with 4.c4 the game becomes the Panov, which is chapter 4. It is a completely different sort of position, so do not treat 4.c4 as a sideline of this chapter.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Recapturing with the queen and losing a move to Nc3. Answering 7.Qb3 by defending b7 with the queen, which leaves Black passive when ...Na5 solves everything. Trading on f3 without a reason and handing White the bishop pair. And treating a symmetrical position as a draw, then drifting: the c-file is a real asset if a rook is put on it.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5
{Recapture with the c-pawn. It keeps a pawn on d5, opens the c-file and leaves the bishop free. [%tags development]}
( 3... Qxd5?
{4.Nc3 develops a piece and hits the queen, so White gets a free move. This is a Scandinavian, and a poor version of one.}
) 4. Bd3 {The main move. White eyes h7 and keeps the c-pawn free for c3. [%weight 7]} (
4. c4
{The Panov. White strikes at d5 straight away and the game changes character completely. [%weight 5]}
4... Nf6
{Develop and defend d5. This is the start of chapter 4. [%tags vs-panov development] [%plan Go to chapter 4. In short: 5.Nc3 e6 and Black blockades the isolated pawn White ends up with after cxd5.] [%line 4.c4, into the Panov]}
) ( 4. Bb5+
{A check for its own sake. It gains no time once the knight blocks. [%weight 3]} 4... Nc6
{Block with a developing move. The knight was going there anyway. [%tags development]} (
4... Bd7!?
{Also fine, and it invites the trade on d7. Blocking with the knight develops the piece that has fewer squares.}
) ( 4... Nd7?!
{It blocks the bishop on c8 and leaves the knight on a worse square than c6.} ) 5. Nf3
{Development, and the knight supports d4. [%weight 6]} 5... Bg4
{Out and active, pinning the knight against the queen. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
6. h3 {White asks the question. [%weight 5]} 6... Bxf3
{Take. White has to recapture with the queen or wreck the pawns, and the knight on f3 was the main defender of d4. [%tags light-squares]}
( 6... Bh5?! {6.g4 Bg6 7.Ne5 gains time and space, and the bishop ends up doing nothing.}
) 7. Qxf3 {The natural recapture, keeping the structure whole. [%weight 8]} 7... e6
{Solid. The bishop that mattered has been traded and Black has an easy game. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Bd6, castle and put a rook on c8. White's bishop on b5 will have to move again, which is another move Black gains.] [%line The 4.Bb5+ check]}
) ( 4. Nf3 {Development, keeping every setup available. [%weight 4]} 4... Nc6
{The usual square, hitting d4. [%tags development]} ( 4... Bg4!?
{Perfectly good and it pins the knight. Developing the knight first keeps ...Bg4 for the moment White commits the bishop.}
) 5. Bd3 {White returns to the main setup. [%weight 6]} 5... Nf6
{Develop and cover d5 and e4, exactly as in the main line. [%tags development] [%plan This is the main line of this chapter by a different order. Expect 6.h3 e6 7.O-O Bd6, or 6.c3 Bg4 with the ...Na5 idea against Qb3.] [%line The plain 4.Nf3]}
) 4... Nc6
{Develop towards the centre and put a second attacker on d4. [%tags development]} (
4... Nf6!?
{Also good, and it usually transposes. The queenside knight is the one with an obvious square.}
) ( 4... Bg4?
{There is nothing on f3 to pin, and the queen sees g4: 5.Qxg4 simply wins the bishop.} )
5. c3
{The standard setup. White props up d4 and gives the queen the c2 square. [%weight 6]} (
5. Nf3 {Development first, leaving the c-pawn where it is for now. [%weight 4]} 5... Nf6
{Develop and keep every option open. [%tags development]} ( 5... Bg4!?
{Fine too, though 6.h3 asks the question immediately and Black has to decide about taking on f3.}
) 6. h3 {White spends a move to keep the bishop off g4. [%weight 5]} 6... e6
{With g4 taken away the bishop has no outside square, so the structure comes first and the bishop goes to d7 later. [%tags development]}
( 6... Bf5?
{It does not get the bishop out, it gives it away: 7.Bxf5 and nothing recaptures, with no pawn on e6 and none on g6. Play ...e6 first.}
) 7. O-O {White castles. [%weight 5]} 7... Bd6
{Get ready to castle and take the diagonal aimed at h2. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Castle, play ...Rc8 or ...Bd7 and ...Rc8, and look for ...Ne4 or the ...e5 break once the pieces are all out.] [%line 5.Nf3 before c3]}
) 5... Nf6 {Develop and cover e4 and d5. [%tags development]} ( 5... g6!?
{A perfectly sound setup with ...Bg7. It is a different plan and it gives up on the quick ...Bg4.}
) ( 5... e6!? {Solid, but it shuts in the bishop on c8 before it has been given a square.}
) 6. Bf4 {White develops the last minor piece to its best diagonal. [%weight 6]} 6... Bg4
{Out it comes, before ...e6 has to be played. The bishop takes aim at the squares White needs for the knight. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]}
( 6... e6!?
{Solid, but it locks in the bishop on c8 for no reason. Getting it out first is the whole idea of the opening.}
) ( 6... Bf5?
{It is not a trade: 7.Bxf5 simply wins the bishop, because with no pawn on e6 and none on g6 nothing takes back. On g4 the bishop is safe.}
) 7. Qb3 {The critical try. The queen hits b7 and takes a look at d5. [%weight 6]} (
7. Nf3 {Quiet development, accepting that the bishop on g4 is there. [%weight 5]} 7... e6
{Complete the structure. The bishop is already outside, so nothing is shut in. [%tags development]}
( 7... Bxf3!?
{It gives White the two bishops for no return. Keep the tension until White does something about it.}
) 8. O-O {White castles into a quiet, level position. [%weight 5]} 8... Bd6
{Offer the trade. Swapping dark-squared bishops suits Black, whose remaining pieces have clear squares. [%tags bishop-placement]}
9. Bxd6 {White takes rather than retreat. [%weight 5]} 9... Qxd6
{Recapture with the queen, which now eyes the kingside and supports ...e5 one day. [%tags development] [%plan Castle short, put a rook on c8 and think about ...e5 once the pieces are out. The position is level and there is nothing to be afraid of.] [%line 7.Nf3 instead of the queen check]}
) 7... Na5
{The move to remember. It attacks the queen and keeps b7 covered by getting out of the way of the rook. [%tags tactics]}
( 7... Qd7?!
{It defends b7 and leaves Black passive with the queen tied down. ...Na5 solves the same problem and gains time.}
) ( 7... e6?
{8.Qxb7 wins the pawn and hits the rook on a8 at the same time, so Black loses time as well. The move guards d5 and forgets what the queen came out for.}
) 8. Qa4+
{The queen keeps the check available and steps away from the knight. [%weight 8]} 8... Bd7
{Block with the bishop, gaining another move on the queen. [%tags development]} (
8... Nc6?!
{It undoes the last move and invites a repetition where White is the one with a choice.} )
9. Qc2 {The queen comes back to the useful diagonal. [%weight 7]} 9... e6
{Now the pawn can come. The bishop is already outside on d7 and has done its job. [%tags development]}
( 9... Nc4?!
{The knight looks active and 10.Bxc4 dxc4 leaves Black with a weak pawn and no compensation.}
) 10. Nf3 {White finally develops the kingside knight. [%weight 7]} 10... Be7
{Quiet development. Note that 10...Bd6 would simply hang a piece: the bishop on d7 blocks the queen, so nothing recaptures after 11.Bxd6. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Castle, then put a rook on c8 and bring the knight back from a5 through c4 or c6. Black has gained two moves on the queen and stands at least equal.] [%line The full main line, with 7.Qb3]}
( 10... Bd6?
{It looks like the usual trade offer and it drops a bishop. 11.Bxd6 cannot be answered, because the queen on d8 is shut in by Black's own bishop on d7.}
) *


[CourseId "caro-kann-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Caro-Kann Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "A solid, well-signposted answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-advance vs-exchange vs-panov vs-fantasy vs-two-knights light-squares c5-break isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterId "ch4-panov"]
[ChapterTitle "The Panov: 4.c4"]
[DefaultTags "vs-panov"]

{White exchanges on d5 and then hits the pawn with 4.c4. This is the most ambitious answer to the Caro-Kann and the one that leads to the least Caro-Kann-like game: open lines, pieces out early, and a fight about one pawn in the middle of the board.

## What an isolated pawn is

Sooner or later White plays cxd5 and Black recaptures with a piece. White is then left with a pawn on d4 and no pawns on c or e to defend it. That is an isolated pawn. It is strong in the middlegame, because it controls c5 and e5 and gives White's pieces space, and it is weak in the endgame, because it has to be defended by pieces forever.

## Black's plan in one line

Blockade it, trade pieces, then win it. The square in front of the pawn, d5, is where a black knight belongs: it cannot be driven away by a pawn. Every pair of pieces that comes off makes the isolated pawn weaker. So Black develops simply, castles, puts a knight on d5 and a rook where it looks at d4, and waits.

## The move order

4...Nf6 5.Nc3 e6 6.Nf3 Be7 is the low-maintenance setup. Black does not commit the queen or the queenside bishop early, and 7.cxd5 Nxd5 reaches the standard picture with the knight already on the blockade square. The sharper 6...Bb4 pins the knight and is perfectly good; it also asks Black to know a lot more.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Taking on c4 and giving up the centre for a pawn that cannot be held. Recapturing on d5 with the e-pawn, which leaves Black with the isolated pawn instead. Blocking the c-file with the queen's knight before the bishops have squares. And trading off the blockading knight for nothing, which sets White's centre free.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6
{Develop and defend d5 at the same time. This is the move that keeps everything simple. [%tags development]}
( 4... dxc4?
{The pawn cannot be held: 5.Bxc4 develops with tempo and White owns the whole centre for nothing.}
) ( 4... e6!?
{Sound, and it usually transposes. Developing a piece first is more useful than a pawn move.}
) ( 4... Nc6!?
{A real line and a sharp one. It invites 5.Nf3 Bg4 with a lot of theory attached.} )
5. Nc3 {The main move. White develops and adds a third attacker to d5. [%weight 8]} (
5. Nf3 {Development before the knight commits to c3. [%weight 4]} 5... e6
{Hold d5 the same way. [%tags development]} ( 5... Nc6!?
{Reasonable, but it blocks the c-file before Black has decided where the pieces go.} )
6. Nc3
{The knight arrives after all, and the position is the main line by transposition. [%weight 7]}
6... Be7
{The usual developing move, ready to castle. [%tags development] [%plan This is the main line of the chapter. Expect 7.cxd5 Nxd5 8.Bd3 O-O and blockade the isolated pawn as usual.] [%line 5.Nf3 before Nc3]}
) ( 5. cxd5
{White resolves the centre at once, which gives Black the easiest game of all. [%weight 3]}
5... Nxd5
{The knight takes the blockade square with a move to spare. [%tags isolated-pawn]} (
5... Qxd5?! {6.Nc3 develops with tempo and White gets the move back.} ) 6. Nf3
{Development. [%weight 6]} 6... e6
{Solid support for the knight on d5. [%tags development]} 7. Nc3
{White challenges the blockading knight. [%weight 6]} 7... Be7
{Develop and castle. If White takes on d5, recapture with the e-pawn and the blockade holds. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, then ...Nc6 and ...b6 with ...Bb7. Trade pieces whenever White offers and aim at the pawn on d4.] [%line 5.cxd5 at once]}
) 5... e6
{Hold the pawn with a pawn. The bishop on c8 is shut in for now, and it comes out to b7 later, which is a better square anyway. [%tags development]}
( 5... g6!?
{A good setup with ...Bg7, and a big body of theory around 6.Qb3 and 6.cxd5. This course takes the quieter road.}
) ( 5... Nc6!?
{Playable, but it blocks the c-file and invites 6.Bg5 with awkward pressure.} ) 6. Nf3
{Simple development. [%weight 6]} ( 6. Bg5
{White pins the knight before finishing development. [%weight 4]} 6... Be7
{Break the pin the simple way. Black is happy to trade a pair of bishops. [%tags development]}
( 6... dxc4?!
{It opens the position while the king is still in the centre and the knight is pinned.} )
7. cxd5 {White takes and accepts the isolated pawn. [%weight 6]} 7... Nxd5
{Recapture with the knight, breaking the pin and taking the blockade square. [%tags isolated-pawn]}
8. Bxe7 {White trades before the knight jumps away. [%weight 7]} 8... Qxe7
{Recapture with the queen, which now looks down the e-file at White's king. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, play ...Nc6 and ...Rd8, and keep trading. A pair of bishops has already gone, which is one step closer to the ending where d4 falls.] [%line The 6.Bg5 pin]}
) ( 6. cxd5 {White takes at once rather than developing first. [%weight 4]} 6... Nxd5
{The knight takes the blockade square immediately. [%tags isolated-pawn]} ( 6... exd5?!
{Again this hands the isolated pawn to Black, which is the wrong side of the bargain.} )
7. Nf3 {Development. [%weight 7]} 7... Be7
{Prepare to castle. Nothing has changed about the plan. [%tags development]} 8. Bc4
{The bishop takes aim at the blockading knight and at f7. [%weight 6]} 8... O-O
{Castle. If White ever trades on d5, the recapture with the e-pawn gives Black a solid extra defender in the centre. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play ...Nc6, ...b6 and ...Bb7, and answer Nxd5 with ...exd5. Trade pieces and head for the ending.] [%line The early 6.cxd5]}
) 6... Be7
{Quiet and flexible. Black gets ready to castle and keeps the option of ...Nc6 or ...b6. [%tags development]}
( 6... Bb4!?
{The main line of the Panov and completely sound. The pin gains time and it comes with far more to remember.}
) ( 6... dxc4?!
{8.Bxc4 hands White a free centre and a developed bishop for a pawn Black cannot keep.} )
7. cxd5 {White releases the tension and takes on the isolated pawn. [%weight 6]} ( 7. Bd3
{White develops and keeps the tension on d5. [%weight 4]} 7... dxc4
{Now taking is right: the bishop has to move again to recapture, so Black gains a move. [%tags tactics]}
8. Bxc4
{The bishop recaptures on a good square, and White has the isolated pawn on d4. [%weight 9]}
8... O-O {Castle and get on with the blockade. [%tags king-safety]} 9. O-O
{White castles as well. [%weight 7]} 9... Nc6
{Develop with pressure on the isolated pawn. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...b6 and ...Bb7, put a knight on d5 when it can stay there, and trade pieces. This is the same plan as the main line with a move to spare.] [%line 7.Bd3 and the ...dxc4 grab]}
) 7... Nxd5
{Recapture with the knight, which lands on the blockade square in front of White's isolated pawn. [%tags isolated-pawn]}
( 7... exd5?!
{It leaves Black with the isolated pawn instead, and the whole plan of the chapter is reversed.}
) 8. Bd3
{The bishop takes the diagonal aimed at h7, which is the point of White's extra space. [%weight 6]}
8... O-O
{Castle before anything else. There is nothing to fear yet, and the king belongs off the centre file. [%tags king-safety]}
( 8... Nc6!?
{Also fine. Castling first means Black is never caught with the king in the middle if the position opens.}
) 9. O-O {White castles too and the middlegame starts. [%weight 8]} 9... Nc6
{Develop with an eye on d4. The isolated pawn now has two attackers and two defenders. [%tags development]}
( 9... Nxc3!?
{It gives White doubled pawns on c3 and a strong centre. Keeping the knight on the blockade square is the plan of this chapter.}
) 10. Re1 {The rook takes the open file and supports a later Ne5 or d5 push. [%weight 5]}
10... Nf6
{Step back. The blockade was never permanent, and from f6 the knight covers h7, which is the square White's bishop and knight have been aiming at all along. [%tags king-safety]}
( 10... b6?
{It looks like the plan and it leaves h7 with nothing guarding it. With the knight still on d5, Bxh7+ followed by Ng5+ and Qh5 is a real attack.}
) ( 10... Nxc3!?
{Playable, and it hands White a broad pawn centre in return for a target. Retreating keeps more pieces on.}
) 11. a3
{A useful move: White takes b4 away from the knight before it ever gets there. [%weight 5]}
11... b6
{Now the bishop can come to b7, because the knight on f6 is holding the kingside together. [%tags development] [%plan Play ...Bb7 and ...Rc8, then watch for White lining a bishop up on c2 with the queen on d3: answer that with ...g6 before it becomes an attack. The pawn on d4 is not going anywhere.] [%line The full main line, with 7.cxd5]}
*


[CourseId "caro-kann-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Caro-Kann Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "A solid, well-signposted answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-advance vs-exchange vs-panov vs-fantasy vs-two-knights light-squares c5-break isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterId "ch5-sidelines"]
[ChapterTitle "Sidelines and move-order tricks"]
[DefaultTags "development"]

{Everything that is not 2.d4 d5 followed by one of the four main choices. None of it is dangerous, and all of it is worth ten minutes so that you are never surprised on move two.

## The Fantasy Variation, 3.f3

White props up e4 with a pawn and hopes for an open f-file later. The critical answer is 3...dxe4 4.fxe4 e5, hitting d4 at once, and it is sharp enough that both sides need to know it. This course plays the quiet 3...e6 instead: build the wall, pin the knight with ...Bb4, trade it off, and only then take on e4. With the f-pawn gone from f3, ...Qh4+ arrives with check and wins the pawn on e4 back at once, and White's king never castles.

## The Two Knights, 2.Nc3 and 3.Nf3

White develops both knights and delays d4. The answer is 3...Bg4, and the point is that the bishop gets out and then trades itself for the knight on f3. Black gives up the bishop pair and gets a completely solid position with no weaknesses, which is a fine bargain when White has no way to open the game.

## The King's Indian Attack, 2.d3

White is not fighting for the centre at all, so take it: 2...d5 3.Nd2 e5. Black has two pawns in the middle and free development, which is more than the Caro-Kann normally promises.

## Transpositions

Most sidelines turn into a main chapter after a move or two. 2.Nc3 d5 3.d4 is chapter 1. 2.Nf3 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.d4 is chapter 2. 2.Nf3 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.d4 is chapter 3. Recognising this is most of the work: play the move you would play there.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Taking on e4 too early against the Fantasy, before ...Bb4 has provoked a weakness. Answering 4.h3 in the Two Knights with ...Bh5, when 5.g4 costs Black the bishop's freedom for nothing. Treating 2.d3 as harmless and playing passively, instead of taking the centre with ...e5. And, generally, playing a sideline by feel rather than steering it into the chapter you already know.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4
{The main move, and everything after it belongs to chapters 1 to 4 unless White tries something unusual on move three. [%weight 8]}
( 2. Nc3
{The start of the Two Knights, and it can also transpose straight back to chapter 1. [%weight 5]}
2... d5
{The same central move as always. There is no reason to change plan. [%tags vs-two-knights]}
( 2... e5!? {Sound, and a completely different game. This repertoire always plays ...d5.}
) 3. Nf3 {The Two Knights proper. White leaves the d-pawn at home. [%weight 6]} ( 3. d4
{Straight back into the main line. [%weight 5]} 3... dxe4
{Take, exactly as in chapter 1. [%plan This is chapter 1. White recaptures with 4.Nxe4 and Black plays 4...Bf5 with the main line.] [%line Two Knights into the main line]}
) ( 3. exd5 {White trades in the centre. [%weight 4]} 3... cxd5
{Recapture with the c-pawn, as always.} 4. d4
{White builds the centre and the game becomes the Exchange with the knight already on c3. [%weight 6]}
4... Nf6
{Develop and hold d5. The knight on c3 is biting on granite. [%plan Play ...Nc6, ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 and ...e6, then castle. Treat it as chapter 3 with White having committed the knight early.] [%line Two Knights into the Exchange]}
) 3... Bg4
{Out, and the bishop is already looking at the knight that holds White's position together. [%tags vs-two-knights bishop-placement]}
( 3... dxe4?!
{4.Nxe4 leaves White with a comfortable game and Black with nothing to show for the released tension.}
) ( 3... Nf6!?
{Reasonable, but 4.e5 gains space. The bishop has one good square here and it should take it.}
) 4. h3 {White asks the question at once. [%weight 6]} 4... Bxf3
{Take. Black gives up the bishop pair and gets a position with no weaknesses and no worries. [%tags light-squares]}
( 4... Bh5?!
{5.g4 Bg6 6.Ne5 gains space with tempo and the bishop ends up doing nothing at all.} )
5. Qxf3 {The natural recapture, keeping the pawns whole. [%weight 9]} 5... Nf6
{Develop and defend d5 a second time.} ( 5... e6!?
{Solid, and it usually transposes. The knight is the piece with the obvious square.} )
6. d3 {A modest move. White has given up on a big centre. [%weight 5]} ( 6. d4
{White builds the centre after all. [%weight 4]} 6... e6
{Solid, and it holds d5 for good. [%plan Play ...Bb4 or ...Be7 and castle. With the light-squared bishops already off, White's attacking chances are small.] [%line Two Knights, with 6.d4]}
) 6... e6 {Build the wall. Black has a solid position and White has nothing to attack.}
7. Be2 {Quiet development. [%weight 5]} 7... Bb4
{Pin the knight and prepare to castle. Trading it would leave White's queenside pawns doubled.}
8. O-O {White castles. [%weight 6]} 8... O-O
{Castle as well. The position is level and easy to play from either side. [%tags king-safety]}
9. Bd2 {White unpins. [%weight 5]} 9... Nbd7
{The last piece comes out. Black is fully developed with a solid structure. [%plan Play ...Qc7 or ...Re8 and think about ...e5 or ...d4. White has the two bishops in a closed position, which is worth very little.] [%line Two Knights, with 6.d3]}
) ( 2. Nf3 {A flexible move that usually transposes to something else. [%weight 4]}
2... d5 {The same reply. Every sideline gets ...d5.} 3. exd5 {White trades. [%weight 5]} (
