{The bishop is heading for g2, which is the whole idea of the setup. [%weight 5]} 5... Nf6
{Develop and hold d5 and e4.} 6. Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto. [%weight 7]}
6... O-O
{Castle. Black has the centre, easy development and nothing to defend. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play ...Re8 and ...Bg4 or ...a5, and hold the centre. If White ever plays exd5, recapture with the c-pawn and enjoy the extra space.] [%line The King's Indian Attack]}
) ( 2. c4 {An early Panov try. Without d4 in first it is less testing. [%weight 2]}
2... d5 {Challenge the centre at once.} 3. exd5 {White takes. [%weight 6]} 3... cxd5
{Recapture with the c-pawn as usual.} 4. cxd5
{White takes again and the pawn on d5 has to be regained. [%weight 8]} 4... Nf6
{Develop and hit the pawn rather than grabbing it with the queen.} 5. Nc3
{White defends the pawn one more time. [%weight 6]} 5... Nxd5
{Take. The knight sits on a fine central square and Black has an easy game. [%plan Play ...Nc6, ...e6 and ...Be7 and castle. Without a pawn on d4 White has no centre at all, so this is a comfortable version of chapter 4.] [%line The 2.c4 Accelerated Panov]}
) 2... d5
{The point of the opening: the pawn arrives supported and White has to make a decision. [%tags light-squares]}
( 2... d6?! {A Pirc where the pawn on c6 is doing very little.} ) ( 2... Nf6?!
{3.e5 gains space and a free move on the knight.} ) 3. f3
{The Fantasy Variation. White holds e4 with a pawn and hopes to build a big centre. [%weight 3]}
( 3. Bd3 {A quiet developing move that gives up the centre. [%weight 2]} 3... dxe4
{Take. The bishop has to move again to recapture, so Black gains a move. [%tags tactics]}
4. Bxe4 {White recaptures with the bishop and stands in the centre. [%weight 8]} 4... Nf6
{Develop and hit the bishop at the same time.} 5. Bf3
{The bishop steps back. Bd3 is the other square and leads to the same sort of position. [%weight 5]}
5... Bf5
{The bishop comes out, which is what this opening is for. White has spent three moves on one bishop and traded it, so Black is already comfortable. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares] [%plan Play ...e6, ...Nbd7 and ...Be7 and castle, then ...c5 or ...Qb6 against d4. Black has an extra developing move and no weaknesses.] [%line The 3.Bd3 sideline]}
( 5... e5?!
{It looks like a free strike in the centre and 6.dxe5 Qxd1+ 7.Kxd1 leaves White the better placed, not Black.}
) ) 3... e6
{The quiet answer. Black builds the wall and gets ready to pin the knight, and the bishop on c8 is not in a hurry. [%tags vs-fantasy]}
( 3... dxe4!?
{The critical test: 4.fxe4 e5 hits d4 at once. It is sound and it is sharp, with real theory attached on both sides.}
) ( 3... g6!?
{A sensible setup with ...Bg7. It leaves White's centre alone, which is exactly what White wants.}
) 4. Nc3 {Development, adding a third defender to e4. [%weight 6]} ( 4. Be3
{White develops before deciding about the centre. [%weight 4]} 4... dxe4
{With no knight on c3 there is nothing to pin, so take at once. [%tags vs-fantasy]}
5. fxe4
{White recaptures and keeps the broad centre. The f-pawn has left f3, which is the detail that matters. [%weight 8]}
5... Qh4+
{The same check as in the main line of this chapter, for the same reason: with f3 empty the queen arrives with check and the pawn on e4 cannot be held. [%tags tactics] [%plan After 6.Kd2 or 6.g3 take on e4, then develop with ...Nf6 and ...Nd7. A pawn and a king stuck in the middle is a good return for one check.] [%line The Fantasy, with 4.Be3]}
( 5... c5?!
{The natural break, and it lets White consolidate the extra centre pawn in peace.} ) )
4... Bb4
{The pin. The knight on c3 is the piece holding e4 together, so Black attacks it before taking. [%tags vs-fantasy]}
( 4... Nf6!?
{Natural development, but it gives up the chance to damage White's queenside pawns.} ) (
4... dxe4!?
{Fine, and it releases the tension before Black has provoked anything. The pin first is more testing.}
) 5. a3 {White asks the bishop to decide. [%weight 5]} ( 5. Bf4
{White develops and leaves the pin alone. [%weight 4]} 5... Ne7
{Develop behind the pawns. The bishop on b4 is not going anywhere and the pin still bites. [%tags vs-fantasy]}
6. Nge2 {The last minor piece comes out. [%weight 5]} 6... O-O
{Castle first and decide about the centre afterwards. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Take on e4 when White has to recapture with a pawn, then hit the centre with ...c5. The pawn on f3 has left White short of squares on the kingside.] [%line The Fantasy, 5.Bf4 instead of a3]}
) 5... Bxc3+
{Take. White's queenside pawns are doubled for the rest of the game, and c3 will not run away. [%tags vs-fantasy tactics]}
6. bxc3
{The only recapture. Blocking with Bd2 or Qd2 instead lets Black take on d2, and White stays a piece down. [%weight 9]}
6... dxe4
{Now take, when the recapture leaves White with a centre that has to be defended by pieces. [%tags vs-fantasy]}
( 6... Nf6?!
{7.e5 hits the knight and the best it can do is go back to g8, so Black gives up the move the knight just made. Taking first settles the centre on Black's terms.}
) 7. fxe4
{White recaptures and has a broad centre, an open f-file and a bare king. [%weight 9]}
7... Qh4+
{The move that makes the whole line worth playing. The pawn on f3 has gone, so the queen reaches h4 with check and the pawn on e4 cannot be held. [%tags tactics]}
( 7... c5?
{The natural break, and it lets White off. 8.Qg4 hits g7 and Black is suddenly the one defending.}
) ( 7... e5?
{It opens the position for White's two bishops with the black king still in the middle, and it passes up the check that wins the pawn back. Nearly two pawns.}
) 8. Ke2
{Forced in practice. 8.g3 loses the rook on h1 after ...Qxe4+ and ...Qxh1. [%weight 6]} (
8. Kd2
{The other way. White keeps the pawn on e4 for a moment and puts the king on an even stranger square. [%weight 4]}
8... Nf6
{Develop and hit e4 again. There is no need to grab anything: White's king is the target. [%plan Play ...Nd7, ...b6 and ...Ba6, and open the position where the king is. If White pushes e5, the knight comes to e4 with check.] [%line The Fantasy, with 8.Kd2]}
) 8... Qxe4+
{Collect the pawn with check. White's king has nowhere good to go and will never castle. [%tags tactics]}
9. Kf2 {The king steps back towards its own pieces. [%weight 8]} 9... Qh4+
{One more check, to make White weaken the kingside before the queen goes home. [%tags tactics]}
10. g3
{Now the pawn has to block, and the dark squares around White's king are permanently loose. [%weight 8]}
10... Qd8
{Home again, a pawn up, with White's king stuck in the middle and the pawns on c3 and g3 to work on. [%plan Develop with ...Nf6, ...Nd7 and ...b6, and castle either side. Do not hurry: White cannot castle, so every quiet move makes the position better.] [%line The Fantasy, with 8.Ke2]}
