[CourseId "caro-kann-black"]
[CourseTitle "The Caro-Kann Defence"]
[StudentColour "black"]
[Tagline "A solid, well-signposted answer to 1.e4, as Black."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-advance vs-exchange vs-panov vs-fantasy vs-two-knights light-squares c5-break isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterId "ch6-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{Four positions, each one the end of a line you already know from an earlier chapter. In every case the last move is Black's, and it is the move the whole opening has been building towards. Play through them until the final move looks obvious, because in a real game it has to.

## The two levers

Black's whole plan in the Caro-Kann comes down to two pawn moves. The first is ...c5, which hits the base of White's pawn chain. The second is ...f6, which hits the head of it. Both need preparation and both need the pieces to be ready to use the lines they open. Everything in between is development.

## Exercise 1: the order of two captures

White has traded off the knight that guarded e5, so the pawn there has no defender at all. Before taking it, look at the bishop on e3. Taking that first, and forcing the f-pawn to recapture, wins the same pawn and wrecks White's kingside into the bargain. The order is the whole exercise.

## Exercise 2: ...c5 in the main line

Opposite side castling, and it is a race. White is going to push pawns at the black king on the kingside. Black opens a file at the white king on the queenside, and gets there first because the lever was ready before White's was. The move is ...c5 and it should never wait.

## Exercise 3: the c-file in the Exchange

A symmetrical position where neither side has a weakness. What Black has is the half open c-file and a way to use it. The exercise ends with the rook arriving on it, because that is the whole plan and most club players never get round to it.

## Exercise 4: pressure on the isolated pawn

The Panov gives White an isolated pawn on d4 and a lot of activity to go with it. That activity comes down to one thing: a bishop and a queen lined up on the diagonal that ends at h7. Spot the battery as it is being built and stop it with one pawn move, and White is left with the weak pawn and nothing else.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Reaching a good position and then having nothing to do, because ...c5 or ...f6 was never prepared. Opening lines before the pieces are ready to use them, which usually helps whoever has more space. Trading the blockading knight for no reason and setting White's centre free. And playing on the wing where the opponent is stronger rather than in the centre where the game is decided.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5
{The Advance. Exercise 1 runs through the main line of chapter 2 and then one move further. [%weight 6]}
( 3. Nc3
{The main line. Exercise 2 follows chapter 1 to the end and then plays the break. [%weight 6]}
3... dxe4 {Take, as always. [%tags development]} 4. Nxe4
{The knight recaptures. [%weight 10]} 4... Bf5
{The bishop steps outside. [%tags bishop-placement light-squares]} 5. Ng3
{The knight retreats with tempo. [%weight 8]} 5... Bg6
{The bishop keeps the diagonal. [%tags bishop-placement]} 6. h4
{The standard pawn advance. [%weight 7]} 6... h6
{Room for the bishop, and no g5 square for White. [%tags king-safety]} 7. Nf3
{Development. [%weight 7]} 7... Nd7 {Covering e5 and leaving f6 free. [%tags development]}
8. h5 {The pawn arrives. [%weight 8]} 8... Bh7
{The only square, and a good enough one. [%tags bishop-placement]} 9. Bd3
{White offers the trade. [%weight 8]} 9... Bxd3
{Take the attacker off the board. [%tags light-squares]} 10. Qxd3
{The queen recaptures. [%weight 10]} 10... e6
{Nothing is blocked in now. [%tags development]} 11. Bd2
{White prepares to castle long. [%weight 5]} 11... Ngf6
