{White develops and eyes the bishop on e7. [%weight 5]} 12... Bb7
{The bishop reaches the long diagonal, where it looks at d5 and past it. [%tags development]}
13. Bc2 {The bishop steps aside, and this is the move to notice. [%weight 5]} 13... Rc8
{The rook takes the open file while White finishes the setup. [%tags development]} 14. Qd3
{There it is: bishop on c2, queen behind it, both aimed at h7. [%weight 6]} 14... g6
{Stop it before it starts. That battery on the b1-h7 diagonal is the only real attack White's isolated pawn buys, and one pawn move takes it away for good. [%plan Now play against d4 with ...Nd5 or ...Bf6 and a rook on d8. With the diagonal shut, every trade brings the ending closer and the isolated pawn is a permanent target.] [%line The Panov: holding h7 with ...g6]}
) 4... Nc6 {Develop with pressure on d4. [%tags development vs-exchange]} 5. c3
